  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 370,
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.edge-core.com/product/ecs4120-28p/",
    "note": "Product page: \"The ECS4120-28P can provide up to 30 Watts of power to attached devices ... The PoE Power Budget is 370W.\" The Web Management Guide R03 states the model as \"24 10/100/1000BASE-T (RJ-45) 802.3af/at PoE Ports, and 4 10-Gigabit SFP+ Ports (PoE Power Budget: 370W)\" (https://www.edge-core.com/_upload/images/ECS4120-EC_WG-R03_2019-02-20.pdf). 24 ports at 30 W would be 720 W, so the 370 W budget is the binding limit: not every port can carry a Class 4 load at once."
  },
  "fabric": {
    "capacityMbps": 128000,
    "kind": "switching fabric",
    "note": "128 Gbps switching capacity and 95 Mpps for the PoE member, from the ECS4120 series datasheet R10 table (https://www.edge-core.com/_upload/images/ECS4120_series_DS_R10_20200108.pdf); the R16 datasheet no longer lists this member."
  },
  "notes": "The PoE member of the family, and the only one: the R16 datasheet (2024) dropped it and the product page records the End of Life process as completed on 1 January 2019 (https://www.edge-core.com/product/ecs4120-28p/), so this spec is a historical member kept for labs that still hold one. Census: 24x 10/100/1000BASE-T PoE RJ-45 plus 4x 10G SFP+ uplinks and 1x RJ-45 console, from Web Management Guide R03 (https://www.edge-core.com/_upload/images/ECS4120-EC_WG-R03_2019-02-20.pdf). The R10 datasheet adds 256 MB flash, 512 MB DRAM, 16 K MAC table, 9 KB jumbo, 460 W maximum system power (370 W PoE plus about 90 W of system), 44 x 33 x 4.4 cm, 4.53 kg. Each copper port is modelled at 30 W (802.3at) because the vendor states 30 W a port, with the 370 W budget capping the total; no separate 802.3af-only figure is published."
}
